In this paper, we consider the canonical setting for a
permuted linear sensing, which is written as
\begin{align}
\label{eq:sys_model}
\bY = \bPi^{\natural}\bX\bB^{\natural} + \bW,
\end{align}
where $\bPi^{\natural} \in \{0, 1\}^{n\times n}$ denotes the unknown permutation matrix,
$\bX \in \RR^{n\times p}$ represents the design (sensing) matrix,
$\bB^{\natural} \in \RR^{p\times m}$
is the signal of interest, and $\bW \in \RR^{n\times m}$ denotes the additive noise.

\begin{align}
\label{eq:sys_model}
\bY = \bPi^{\natural}\bX\bB^{\natural} + \bW,
\end{align}

